# Local dev environment (Lukas's Mac — NOT the CoreWeave RL agent)

AGENTS.md is for the CoreWeave orchestrator agent. THIS file is the
local-machine knowledge: venv, sim viewer, checkpoint pulling.

## Python environment — repo root `.venv` from `pyproject.toml` + `uv.lock`

The repo root `pyproject.toml` declares every dependency (CAD pipeline,
MuJoCo sim + RL, robot bench tools, vision) and `uv.lock` pins them.
Python is **3.12** (build123d and hexapod-tracker both need it; uv
installs it on demand). One command creates or repairs the env:

```sh
cd ~/hexapod && uv sync          # -> /Users/lukas/hexapod/.venv ; idempotent
uv sync --group mjx              # + JAX/MJX backend (parity tests, bench_mjx)
uv sync --group fea              # + gmsh/pygmsh/meshio for make check-strength
```

`uv run ...` picks that env up from ANY directory in the checkout (no
direnv, no activation, no `VIRTUAL_ENV`). The prototype packages are
installed editable with `hexapod_walker/prototype_sts3215`,
`linux_control`, `motor_setup`, and `rl_move/orchestrator` on the import
path, so `import rl_move`, `import hexapod_core`, `import hexapod_prototype`
and the bare-module style (`import feetech_bus`, `import safe_zero`) all
work without `sys.path` shims.

- **Adding a dependency:** edit `pyproject.toml`, run `uv lock`, then
  `uv sync`. Never `uv pip install` into the venv — it is not recorded and
  disappears at the next sync.
- **Worktrees get their OWN `.venv`** (`uv sync` in the worktree; seconds,
  hard-linked from the uv cache). The editable install points at the
  checkout it was synced in, so sharing the main checkout's venv would make
  a worktree import the main checkout's code. See `agent-worktrees.mdc`.
- The old `requirements.txt` and the ad hoc `uv pip install ...` recipes are
  gone; `pygame-ce` (gamepad), `ftservo-python-sdk` (Feetech bus),
  `manifold3d` (trimesh booleans — without it the CAD silently emits parts
  with NO bolt holes) are all declared.
- Remote runtimes do NOT use this project: pods get a tarball of
  `prototype_sts3215/` and their own pins (`bootstrap_train_pod.sh`), the
  Uno Q gets the staged deploy tree with `PYTHONPATH` from systemd, and the
  controller pod runs with `UV_NO_PROJECT=1`.

**Never `rm -rf .venv` without the user asking.** `uv sync` repairs a
broken env in place; there is no reason to delete it. (It was nuked once,
2026-08-09, by an agent whose `ls` existence check silently aborted on a
zsh no-match glob — check with `ls -d ~/hexapod/.venv`, no globs.)

## Local MuJoCo viewer — trying RL policies on the Mac

Champion checkpoints live on the CoreWeave pods, NOT in git. Pull with:

```sh
KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/coreweave.yaml kubectl cp \
  <pod>:/workspace/prototype_sts3215/rl_move/sim/policies/<name>.zip \
  hexapod_walker/prototype_sts3215/rl_move/sim/policies/<name>.zip
# or: rl_move/orchestrator/ops.sh pullckpt <run>
```

Verify md5 against RL_LOG.md. Champions as of 2026-08-09 (both already
pulled locally): walk = `ppo_goal_cw_walk_longdist_r2.zip` (bcddc65c…),
stance/heights = `ppo_goal_cw_stance_dr10.zip` (da1d912a…). Not every
pod has every zip — `train-0` and `hexapod-sweep-friction` are pack rats.

**USE THE SCRIPTS — do not hand-assemble viewer commands.** They live
in `hexapod_walker/prototype_sts3215/sim_viewer/` — **READ
`sim_viewer/README.md` FIRST**, it documents the whole local-sim
workflow (checkpoints, obs layouts, pose gap, interpreter rules):

```sh
sim_viewer/sim_play.sh     # THE ONE TO USE: both champions in one sim.
                           # 7 = stand up (auto), I/K/J/L = walk,
                           # Space = stop, 8 = sit, 9 = reset, Q = quit.
sim_viewer/sim_stand.sh    # stance champion alone (mujoco.viewer window)
sim_viewer/sim_walk.sh     # walk champion alone (cv2 drive window)
```

The canonical local browser/control surface on the Mac is the managed
`:8898` hub:

```sh
cd ~/hexapod/hexapod_walker/prototype_sts3215
make web-8898-start       # http://localhost:8898/rl
make web-8898-status
make web-8898-restart
make web-8898-stop
```

It runs `uv run python -m rl_move.sim.web_server` under `launchctl` via
`sim_viewer/hexapod_web_8898.sh`, resolves the robot's current `:8080`
target unless `HEXAPOD_HOST` is set, and logs to
`/tmp/hexapod_web_8898.log`. Do not hand-assemble direct
`.venv/bin/python`, `nohup`, or random-worktree launch commands for
`8898`.

Key bindings are drawn IN the window (the user cannot see the terminal
while a viewer has focus). `sim_play.sh` runs `rl_move/sim/play.py`, a
cv2 window — cv2 has NO built-in key bindings, so there is no clash
with MuJoCo's letter toggles (in `mujoco.viewer`, almost every letter
is a display toggle; that's why view.py's bindings are digits-only).

Gotchas encoded in the scripts (learned the hard way — don't re-derive):

- Champion checkpoints are 18-action per-joint policies → `view.py`
  needs **`--task joint_goal`** (obs 68). The default `--task goal` is
  the 56-obs body-offset env and dies with "Unexpected observation
  shape (56,)".
- Walk env obs (72) = stance obs (68) + [vx_ref, vy_ref, vx_meas,
  vy_meas] appended — so the stance policy can run inside the walk env
  on `obs[:68]`. That's how play.py runs both in one sim.
- **The two champions stand DIFFERENTLY**: stance's belly rise ends in
  a ~72 mm crouch-stand; the walk champion lives around the ~142 mm
  plant stance (joint gap to 104 deg). Handing straight from one to the
  other collapses into a belly shuffle (measured). play.py's `7` key
  bridges it: stance rise → scripted 1.5 s joint blend to the plant
  pose → episode re-anchor in the plant frame.
- `view.py` must run under **mjpython** (it self-re-execs on macOS; do
  not "fix" that). `drive_policy`/`play` must run under **plain
  python** — cv2 render path; mjpython segfaults intermittently.
- The ~5 s belly curl before the stand-up ramp is the training profile,
  not a hang.
- Height refs are RELATIVE to the episode's start pose: belly-start
  episodes stand at +45 mm; plant-start episodes crouch at −60 mm.

## Robot on the bench (quick refs)

- Robot web API / UI: `http://hexapod.local:8080` (mDNS; works on any
  LAN). HTTP preferred over SSH; SSH `arduino@hexapod.local` (key auth)
  for deploy only when asked. `linux_control/wifi_join.sh` joins the
  board to a new WiFi over USB adb (auto mode matches the Mac's saved
  networks via the System keychain).
- Laptop → FE-URT USB serial: `/dev/tty.usbmodem*`, e.g.
  `python motor_setup/feetech_bus.py --port /dev/tty.usbmodem5B790331181 scan`
  (expect IDs 2..19; ID 1 = factory-fresh servo).
